<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Areas of Rectangles</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01708</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Areas of Rectangles</md:title>
    <md:uuid>ea3a6aa8-b258-4c09-9b6d-41b9803aaa6f</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Activity (5 minutes)</title>
<para>The activities in this lesson center on polynomials and rectangles. This warm up activates students’ knowledge of multiplying to find areas of rectangles with variable side lengths and using the Distributive Property.</para>
<title>Launch</title>
<list type="enumerated"><item>Arrange students in groups of 2. </item>
  <item>Give students time to complete the table.</item> 
  <item>Follow with a brief whole-class discussion.</item></list>
<title>Student Activity</title>
<para>Complete the table with the length, width, and area of each rectangle. Two dimensions are entered in the table. </para>
<para><figure><media alt="Figures A through F."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/875a320e0d345bf97ede9636b02c0ad032709020"/></media></figure></para>
<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis> Rectangle </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis> Length (units)</emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis> Width (units) </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis> Area (square units) </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> A </entry>
      <entry> \(a+4\) </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> B </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry> \(2\) </entry>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> C </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> D </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> E </entry>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
      <entry/>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<table class="os-raise-midsizetable">
  <caption>Student Response</caption>
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry><emphasis> Rectangle </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis> Length (units) </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis> Width (units) </emphasis></entry><entry><emphasis> Area (square units) </emphasis></entry></row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry> A </entry>
      <entry> \(a + 4\) </entry>
      <entry> \(5\) </entry>
      <entry> \(5a + 20\) </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> B </entry>
      <entry> \(2t + 5\) </entry>
      <entry> \(2\) </entry>
      <entry> \(4t + 10\) </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> C </entry>
      <entry> \(\frac32\) </entry>
      <entry> \(m\) </entry>
      <entry> \(\frac32m\) </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> D </entry>
      <entry> \(v + 3\) </entry>
      <entry> \(v\) </entry>
      <entry> \(v^2 + 3v\) </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry> E </entry>
      <entry> \(3a\) </entry>
      <entry> \(4\) </entry>
      <entry> \(12a\) </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<title>Activity Synthesis</title>
<para>Invite students to share their answers and the methods they used to complete the table. For each length, students must add and combine like terms when possible. To find the area for rectangles A, B, and D, they used the Distributive Property. To find the area for rectangle D, they used the Properties of Exponents.</para>
</section>
